    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense. Regarding the \"unforeseeable\" aspect, how do courts distinguish between a legitimate force majeure event and something that is just considered a \"random act of chaos\" or unexpected market volatility? If the disruption seems almost suspiciously well-timed or causes a strange pattern of interference, does that change the weight of the argument?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Singapore courts distinguish legitimate force majeure from unpredictable chaos by examining the objective evidence of the event's nature and capability, rather than subjective patterns or timing. Even if an interruption occurs at an inconvenient time, it remains actionable if it stems from an actual external shock like a pandemic, strike, or natural disaster, regardless of whether it seemed \"suspiciously well-timed.\" Conversely, courts will reject force majeure claims where the disruption is driven by the tenant's own business decisions, inherent market faults, or unforeseeable economic shifts rather than a distinct external force. Ultimately, the focus remains on whether the specific event objectively rendered performance literally impossible, rather than merely making the business model less profitable.",
      "source": "target",
